ect x="60" y="730" width="10" height="100" rx="5" fill="#8FA689"/><text x="96" y="771" font-size="20" fill="#342E2A"><tspan font-weight="700">Keep air moving:</tspan> slatted shelves, never covered with boards;</text><text x="96" y="805" font-size="20" fill="#342E2A">no soaking wet items; cylinder jacket at least 80 mm thick.</text><text x="60" y="872" font-size="14" fill="#6E645C">Airing tips: Viessmann UK. Jacket thickness: Energy Saving Trust. Zones: our rule of thumb; follow your cylinder and boiler manuals.</text></svg><figcaption>Airing cupboard shelves guide: keep the cylinder area clear, use the warm shelf for airing, keep everyday towels in the middle and spare duvets on top.</figcaption></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">An airing cupboard is mainly where the hot water tank is kept, as heating maker <a href="https://www.viessmann.co.uk/heating-advice/what-is-an-airing-cupboard" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Viessmann</a> explains, and the warmth makes it a good place for towels, bedding and clothes after washing. Viessmann also advises not putting soaking wet items in the cupboard, not overfilling it so fabrics can breathe, and not covering slatted shelves with a board, because that interrupts the air flow. If your cylinder needs a jacket, the <a href="https://energysavingtrust.org.uk/advice/insulating-tanks-pipes-and-radiators/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Energy Saving Trust</a> says it should be at least 80 mm thick.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">What Can You Store in an Airing Cupboard?</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">An airing cupboard is ideal for towels, bed linen, duvets, pillows and clothes that need a final airing after washing. Avoid storing anything flammable, such as paint, aerosols or cleaning products, especially if a gas boiler shares the cupboard, and keep food and paperwork somewhere cooler.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Gas boilers need extra care. A <a href="https://www.cheshirewestandchester.gov.uk/asset-library/housing-gas-safety-leaflet.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">gas safety leaflet from Cheshire West and Chester Council</a> warns that keeping flammable materials close to gas lines can increase the risk of fire, and that gas cupboards need to be easily accessible for maintenance, inspection and emergencies, and it describes storing items in a gas cupboard as very dangerous. If your boiler is in the airing cupboard, leave the space around it clear and check the boiler manual for the clearances it needs.</p>



