<h2 class="wp-block-heading">How to Fit Airing Cupboard Shelves in 6 Steps</h2>



<ol class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Empty the cupboard.</strong> Take everything out and check for leaks, damp patches or a dripping valve before you add shelves.</li>



<li><strong>Note what must stay reachable.</strong> The cylinder, pipes, valves, the immersion heater switch and any boiler controls.</li>



<li><strong>Measure.</strong> Width, depth and height, and the height of the top of the cylinder.</li>



<li><strong>Choose slatted shelves.</strong> Planed timber slats with small gaps let warm air rise through the stacks.</li>



<li><strong>Fix battens to the walls.</strong> Check for hidden pipes and cables first, then screw timber battens to the side and back walls, level, and rest or screw the slatted shelves on top.</li>



<li><strong>Load it by zone.</strong> Airing shelf just above the cylinder, everyday towels in the middle, spares on top.</li>
</ol>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Airing Cupboard Shelf Ideas</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">These airing cupboard shelf ideas give you more storage without blocking the warmth or the parts a plumber needs to reach.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">1. Fit Slatted Pine Shelves</h3>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/airing-cupboard-02-slatted-shelves.jpg" alt="Slatted pine airing cupboard shelves on wooden battens holding neat stacks of folded white, oatmeal and sage green towels and sheets" class="wp-image-2122" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/airing-cupboard-02-slatted-shelves.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/airing-cupboard-02-slatted-shelves-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/airing-cupboard-02-slatted-shelves-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/airing-cupboard-02-slatted-shelves-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Slatted shelves let air reach your linen. Warm air from the cylinder rises through the gaps, so towels stay fresh, and the slats are cheap to make from planed pine. Leave a small gap between each slat and sand the edges so they do not snag your linen.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">2. Run Battens Around Three Walls</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Screwing battens to the back and both side walls makes a shelf that is supported on three sides and will not sag under a pile of towels. Use a level, check for hidden pipes and cables with a detector first (airing cupboard walls often hide them), then screw into solid wall or studs. Our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/diy-closet-shelves/">DIY cupboard shelves guide</a> explains cleat shelves step by step.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">3. Start the Shelves Above the Cylinder</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The lowest shelf should sit above the top of the cylinder, not around it. That keeps the pipes, valves and immersion heater switch easy to reach and leaves a clear space for a plumber to work. Cut a notch in the back slats only where a pipe has to pass, rather than boxing the pipes in.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">4. Plan Shelves Around a Boiler With Care</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Gas safety advice is to avoid storing things in a gas boiler cupboard at all. If a combi or system boiler hangs in a larger airing cupboard, keep shelves well away from it and from its flue, pipes and controls. Boiler manuals list the service clearances needed around the case, so check yours before you fit anything, and leave the area under the boiler empty.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">5. Use a Freestanding Slatted Unit</h3>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/airing-cupboard-06-freestanding-unit.jpg" alt="Freestanding slatted pine shelving unit standing inside a small cupboard, holding folded towels, bedding and a basket, with space on both sides" class="wp-image-2126" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/airing-cupboard-06-freestanding-unit.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/airing-cupboard-06-freestanding-unit-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/airing-cupboard-06-freestanding-unit-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/airing-cupboard-06-freestanding-unit-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Renting, or not allowed to drill? A freestanding slatted shelving unit gives you the same airy storage and moves with you. Choose one narrow enough to leave a clear gap on each side and keep it away from the cylinder and pipes.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">6. Go Floor to Ceiling After a Combi Swap</h3>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/airing-cupboard-04-full-height-shelves.jpg" alt="Narrow landing cupboard with a sage green door open and floor-to-ceiling white oak slatted shelves holding towels, bedding and pillows, with no hot water tank inside" class="wp-image-2124" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/airing-cupboard-04-full-height-shelves.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/airing-cupboard-04-full-height-shelves-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/airing-cupboard-04-full-height-shelves-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/airing-cupboard-04-full-height-shelves-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Many homes lose their hot water cylinder when a combi boiler is fitted, which leaves an empty cupboard on the landing. Fill it with evenly spaced slatted shelves from floor to ceiling and it becomes a proper linen cupboard. Without the cylinder it will not be heated, so keep the slats and leave room for air.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Airing Cupboard Storage Ideas</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Once the shelves are in, these airing cupboard storage ideas keep everything easy to find.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">7. Store Bedding Sets Inside a Pillowcase</h3>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/airing-cupboard-03-bedding-sets.jpg" alt="Airing cupboard shelf with bedding sets folded inside matching pillowcases beside woven baskets of rolled hand towels and flannels" class="wp-image-2123" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/airing-cupboard-03-bedding-sets.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/airing-cupboard-03-bedding-sets-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/airing-cupboard-03-bedding-sets-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/airing-cupboard-03-bedding-sets-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Fold a duvet cover and fitted sheet, then slip them inside one of the matching pillowcases. Each set becomes one neat bundle, so you never hunt for the right pillowcase again. Our guide on <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/how-to-fold-clothes-to-save-space/">how to fold clothes to save space</a> shows the folds that keep stacks tidy.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">8. Give Each Shelf One Job</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Towels on one shelf, bedding on the next, spare pillows on top. When every shelf has a single job, the cupboard stays tidy because everyone knows where things go back.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">9. Roll Hand Towels and Flannels</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Small towels and flannels topple off a stack. Roll them and stand them in an open basket so you can grab one without pulling the rest out. Open baskets also let air reach the fabric.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">10. Keep Spare Duvets in Cotton Bags</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Bulky spare duvets and pillows belong on the top shelf in breathable cotton bags, which keep dust off while letting the filling breathe. Avoid sealing them in plastic in a warm cupboard.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">11. Use the Back of the Door</h3>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/airing-cupboard-05-door-rack.jpg" alt="Shallow white rack on the inside of a cream cupboard door holding a basket of rolled hand towels and spare toilet rolls, with towels on shelves inside" class="wp-image-2125" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/airing-cupboard-05-door-rack.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/airing-cupboard-05-door-rack-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/airing-cupboard-05-door-rack-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/airing-cupboard-05-door-rack-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A shallow over-door rack (no screws needed) on the inside of the door holds rolled hand towels, spare toilet rolls or a lint roller. Check the rack does not hit the shelves when the door closes. For a larger linen store, see our guide to <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/linen-closet-organization/">organising a linen store</a>.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">12. Sort by Room or by Person</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">In a busy home, give each bedroom or each person a stack: one for the main bedroom, one for the children and one for guests. It makes changing beds much quicker.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Airing and Warmth Ideas</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">An airing cupboard is warm, but it only works well if air can move and the heat is not wasted.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">13. Finish Off Slightly Damp Items</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The shelf just above the cylinder is the warmest. Use it to finish off towels or clothes that are only slightly damp after the tumble dryer or washing line. Soaking wet washing adds moisture to the cupboard and can leave it smelling musty.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">14. Never Cover the Slats</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">It is tempting to lay a board over slatted shelves to stop small items falling through. Use a basket instead, because a solid board blocks the warm air that keeps the linen fresh.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">15. Leave Room for Air to Move</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A cupboard packed to the door has no air flow. Leave a little space between stacks and at the back of each shelf. If the cupboard smells damp, take everything out, look for a leak and air it with the door open.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">16. Insulate the Cylinder</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">An uninsulated cylinder heats the cupboard but wastes energy. If your cylinder has a jacket, the <a href="https://energysavingtrust.org.uk/advice/insulating-tanks-pipes-and-radiators/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Energy Saving Trust</a> says it should be at least 80 mm thick. The cupboard will be a little cooler, but less heat is wasted.</p>



<div class="dsh-save" style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:center;gap:24px;background:#F7F1E9;border:1px solid #E6DCCF;border-radius:16px;padding:24px;margin:2em 0;"><a href="https://www.pinterest.com/pin/create/button/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fdailyspacehacks.com%2Fairing-cupboard-shelves%2F&amp;media=https%3A%2F%2Fcdn.jsdelivr.net%2Fgh%2Fmohamedelber%2Fdsh-pins%40main%2Fsrc%2Fairing-cupboard%2Fpins%2Fpin-airing-cupboard-5-checklist.jpg&amp;description=Airing%20cupboard%20checklist%3A%20what%20goes%20where%2C%20from%20the%20tank%20up" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow" style="flex:0 0 200px;max-width:200px;margin:0 auto;"><img decoding="async" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/pin-airing-cupboard-5-checklist.jpg" data-pin-media="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/mohamedelber/dsh-pins@main/src/airing-cupboard/pins/pin-airing-cupboard-5-checklist.jpg" alt="Airing cupboard checklist: keep the tank and pipes clear, warm shelf for slightly damp items, everyday towels in the middle and spare duvets on top" width="200" style="width:200px;height:auto;border-radius:10px;display:block;box-shadow:0 4px 14px rgba(0,0,0,0.12);"/></a><div style="flex:1 1 260px;"><p style="font-size:1.35em;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 8px;color:#342E2A;">Save the checklist</p><p style="margin:0 0 12px;color:#6E645C;">Pin the four zones so you know what goes where the next time you sort your airing cupboard.</p><a href="https://www.pinterest.com/pin/create/button/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fdailyspacehacks.com%2Fairing-cupboard-shelves%2F&amp;media=https%3A%2F%2Fcdn.jsdelivr.net%2Fgh%2Fmohamedelber%2Fdsh-pins%40main%2Fsrc%2Fairing-cupboard%2Fpins%2Fpin-airing-cupboard-5-checklist.jpg&amp;description=Airing%20cupboard%20checklist%3A%20what%20goes%20where%2C%20from%20the%20tank%20up" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="display:inline-block;background:#E60023;color:#FFFFFF;font-weight:700;padding:12px 22px;border-radius:999px;text-decoration:none;white-space:nowrap;line-height:1.2;">Save to Pinterest</a></div></div>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Common Airing Cupboard Mistakes</h2>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Boxing in pipes and valves.</strong> A plumber needs to reach them quickly if something leaks.</li>



<li><strong>Solid shelves or boards over slats.</strong> They stop warm air rising through the linen.</li>



<li><strong>Soaking wet washing.</strong> It adds moisture and can make the cupboard smell musty.</li>



<li><strong>Storing flammable items.</strong> Paint, aerosols and cleaning products do not belong in a warm cupboard or near a gas boiler.</li>



<li><strong>Overfilling every shelf.</strong> Packed linen cannot breathe.</li>



<li><strong>Ignoring drips.</strong> A small leak from a valve or the cylinder can damage floors and ceilings below.</li>
</ul>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Airing Cupboard FAQ</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Should airing cupboard shelves be slatted?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Yes. Slatted shelves let warm air from the hot water cylinder rise through your towels and bedding, which keeps them dry and fresh. Solid shelves block that air flow, so if small items fall through the gaps, use an open basket rather than covering the slats with a board.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">How far apart should airing cupboard shelves be?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">About 30 to 35 cm between shelves suits stacks of towels and folded bedding, and a little more on the top shelf for duvets and pillows. This is our rule of thumb, so measure your tallest stack first. Our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/shelf-spacing-calculator/">shelf spacing calculator</a> works out the gaps for you.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Can you put shelves around a boiler?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Ideally, no. Gas safety advice says storing items in a gas boiler cupboard can be dangerous, and the boiler must stay easy to reach. If the boiler shares a large cupboard, keep shelves well away from the case, flue, pipes and controls, leave the space below it empty and check the boiler manual for clearances.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Can you store towels next to a hot water cylinder?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Yes, towels and bedding are exactly what an airing cupboard is for. Keep them on shelves above the cylinder rather than piled against it, so the cylinder, pipes and valves stay easy to reach and air can move around the linen.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Final Thoughts</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Good airing cupboard shelves are simple: slatted, fixed on battens, starting above the cylinder and loaded by zone. Keep the pipes reachable, the slats uncovered and the shelves not too full, and your towels and bedding will always come out warm and fresh. For more UK storage ideas, see our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/boot-room-ideas/">boot room ideas</a> and <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/small-utility-room-ideas/">small utility room ideas</a>.</p>



